            <div xml:id="d108">

                <pb facs="0212" n="117" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg117"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13470: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-21T16:00:00+08:00">April 21, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 21—11:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>204. The Consul General at Mukden<anchor n="55" xml:id="d108-f1"/> reported in
                    despatch 247, April 14,<anchor n="56" xml:id="d108-f2"/> that he had advised
                    Americans residing at Sinpin to <pb facs="0213" n="118" xml:id="pg118"/>
                    evacuate. Prior to issuing the warning, Ballantine had been informed by the
                    commander of the gendarmes at Mukden that banditry was rife in the Sinpin area
                    and that there were not enough troops available to afford adequate protection. A
                    part of the danger apparently arose from the attitude of the authorities toward
                    the missionaries at Sinpin, some evidence of which is contained in Mukden’s
                    despatch 234, March 12.<anchor n="56a" xml:id="d108-f3"/> It is understood that
                    of the five or six Americans who were at Sinpin, only one or two remain and that
                    those remaining expect to evacuate within 1 or 2 days.</p>
                <p>By mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
